  :adr/title "Religious-Community Mental Model Framework: Cross-Tradition Comparison Schema"
  :adr/status "draft"
  :adr/date-created "2026-07-18"
  :adr/authors ["Jun Kawasaki"]
  :adr/body "## Summary

Define a unified Datomic/DataScript schema and EDN framework to represent religious organizations, secular communities, and their mental models (governance, doctrine, actor structure, lexicons) in a comparable way. Build on etzhayyim's existing 11-axis evaluation system, extend to 14 universal axes, and instantiate 12 diverse organizations (7 religious + 5 secular) for analytical comparison.

## Problem

etzhayyim is a synthetic religion unifying Japanese (八百万/縁起/産霊/和) and Protestant Christian (Sola Scriptura/万人祭司/Reformed Just War/Tree of Life) values. Its mental model — 10 axes (selfproduction/metabolism/homeostasis/active inference/reproduction/symbiosis/diversity/wellbecoming/antifragility/sanctification) + governance gates + Tier-A/B/C actors — is sophisticated and effective.

However:
1. etzhayyim is the only instance. Its framework is specific to one synthetic religion.
2. No comparative schema exists to ask: \"What mental model dimensions do *different* religious traditions naturally define?\", \"How do governance models (hierarchical vs consensus) correlate with doctrine-amendment authority?\", \"Which actor patterns are universal across traditions, and which are tradition-specific?\"
3. Secular communities (labor unions, cooperatives, universities) have similar structural needs (governance, mission, doctrine-like foundational beliefs, role clarity) but are excluded from analysis.
4. Cross-tradition doctrinal conflicts and lexical nuance are not formally captured.

## Decision

Create a shared EDN/Datomic schema instantiating:

### Entities & Attributes (Core)

**`:org/*` (religious-org / secular-community)**
- `:org/id` (keyword, unique)
- `:org/name` (string)
- `:org/type` (:religious-corp | :secular-community | :faith-based-org)
- `:org/tradition` (ref to `:tradition/*`, e.g., Christian/Reformed, Buddhist/Theravada, Labor/Syndicalist)
- `:org/founding-date` (long, year)
- `:org/member-count` (long, estimated)
- `:org/governance-model` (:hierarchical | :democratic | :consensus | :meritocratic)
- `:org/charter` (ref to `:charter/*` or blob)
- `:org/mission-statement` (string)
- `:org/core-beliefs` (ref `:doctrine/*`, many)
- `:org/tier-a-actors` (ref `:actor/*`, many) — governance/leadership
- `:org/tier-b-actors` (ref `:actor/*`, many) — operations/mission-delivery
- `:org/tier-c-cells` (string description) — small groups/grassroots
- `:org/primary-lexicons` (ref `:lexicon/*`, many)

**`:mental-model-axis/*` (14-axis evaluation framework)**

Initialize with 14 axes (etzhayyim's 11 + 3 generic complements):

1. `:axis-governance` — Council/representative legitimacy (0–100, how many decision-seats filled?)
2. `:axis-economic` — Sustainable funding pipeline (0–100, treasury health)
3. `:axis-identity` — Member identity coverage (0–100, DID/SBT/formal membership density)
4. `:axis-agency` — Organizational agency (0–100, LLM agent infrastructure vs human-only leadership)
5. `:axis-social-delivery` — Real-world service/care delivery (0–100, social coverage)
6. `:axis-physical-presence` — Physical infrastructure (0–100, robotics/hospitals/temples/HQs)
7. `:axis-public-trust` — Community trust (0–100, external perception)
8. `:axis-legal-contract` — Legal/constitutional maturity (0–100, formalized charter)
9. `:axis-land-resource` — Land/physical-asset stewardship (0–100, trust registry)
10. `:axis-education` — Knowledge/doctrinal transmission (0–100, seminary/schools/study groups)
11. `:axis-dispute-resolution` — Conflict resolution mechanisms (0–100, formal process)
12. `:axis-external-recognition` — Peer/institutional recognition (0–100, new: secular organizations measure differently)
13. `:axis-member-engagement` — Active participation rate (0–100, new: generic across all)
14. `:axis-doctrine-clarity` — Doctrinal/ideological coherence (0–100, new: tradition-specific weight)

**`:org-axis/*` (instantiate axis for each org)**
- `:org-axis/org` (ref `:org/*`)
- `:org-axis/axis` (ref `:axis/*`)
- `:org-axis/current-value` (long, 0–100)
- `:org-axis/measurement-date` (string, ISO 8601)
- `:org-axis/measurement-basis` (string: simulation | survey | document-analysis | assumption)
- `:org-axis/caveat` (string, limitations)

**`:doctrine/*` (belief systems, foundational ideas)**
- `:doctrine/id` (keyword, unique)
- `:doctrine/name` (string)
- `:doctrine/tradition` (ref `:tradition/*`)
- `:doctrine/category` (:cosmology | :soteriology | :eschatology | :ethics | :anthropology | :economic-justice | :governance)
- `:doctrine/origin-text` (string, e.g., \"John 1:1\", \"Surah Al-Fatiha:1\", \"Four Noble Truths\")
- `:doctrine/summary` (string, ~200 words)
- `:doctrine/conflicting-with` (ref `:doctrine/*`, many) — which other tradition's doctrines conflict?
- `:doctrine/historical-evolution` (string)

**`:actor-pattern/*` (roles, governance structure)**
- `:actor/id` (keyword)
- `:actor/name` (string)
- `:actor/belongs-to-org` (ref `:org/*`)
- `:actor/tier` (:tier-a-governance | :tier-b-operational | :tier-c-cell)
- `:actor/authority` (:decision | :advisory | :execution | :community)
- `:actor/primary-role` (string, e.g., \"Council member\", \"Parish priest\", \"Union steward\")
- `:actor/team-size` (long, 1-based)
- `:actor/reporting-to` (ref `:actor/*`, many, supports matrix structures)
- `:actor/key-responsibilities` (string, many)

**`:charter/*` (constitutional/foundational documents)**
- `:charter/id` (keyword)
- `:charter/org` (ref `:org/*`)
- `:charter/name` (string)
- `:charter/date-adopted` (string, ISO 8601)
- `:charter/amendment-process` (string)
- `:charter/authority-level` (:immutable | :lv7-unanimity | :simple-majority | :governor-discretion)
- `:charter/full-text-blob` (string, markdown/prose)
- `:charter/key-sections` (ref `:charter-section/*`, many)

**`:lexicon/*` (tradition-specific terminology)**
- `:lexicon/id` (keyword)
- `:lexicon/name` (string)
- `:lexicon/language` (keyword, :en | :ja | :ar | :he | :sa | :pi)
- `:lexicon/tradition` (ref `:tradition/*`)
- `:lexicon/scope` (:ritual | :doctrinal | :organizational | :daily-practice)
- `:lexicon/entries` (ref `:lexicon-entry/*`, many)

**`:lexicon-entry/*`**
- `:lexicon-entry/term` (string, original language)
- `:lexicon-entry/pronunciation` (string, if applicable)
- `:lexicon-entry/definition` (string)
- `:lexicon-entry/usage-context` (string)
- `:lexicon-entry/cognates` (string, related terms in other languages/traditions)

**`:tradition/*` (high-level tradition categorization)**
- `:tradition/id` (keyword, :christian-reformed, :islamic-sunni, :buddhist-theravada, etc.)
- `:tradition/name` (string)
- `:tradition/parent-tradition` (ref `:tradition/*`, optional, e.g., Christian → Christian/Reformed)
- `:tradition/founding-approx-year` (long)
- `:tradition/major-doctrines` (ref `:doctrine/*`, many)
- `:tradition/primary-languages` (keyword, many)
- `:tradition/estimated-members` (long)

**`:region/*` (geographic reference)**
- `:region/id` (keyword)
- `:region/name` (string)
- `:region/parent-region` (ref `:region/*`, optional)

## Sample Instantiation (12 organizations)

**Religious (7)**:
1. etzhayyim (synthetic Christian/Jewish, 2026, SBT-council governance)
2. Lutheran-Reformation (Christian/Reformed, ~1520, synodal democracy)
3. Orthodox-Christian (Christian/Orthodox, ~1054, hierarchical Patriarch-Synod)
4. Sunni-Islam (Islamic/Sunni, ~1981 modern ISNA, shura council)
5. Shia-Islam (Islamic/Shia, ~1500s, Ayatollah hierarchy)
6. Theravada-Buddhism (Buddhist/Theravada, ~2500 yrs, monastic-sangha)
7. Mahayana-Buddhism (Buddhist/Mahayana, ~2000 yrs, temple-hierarchical)

**Secular (5)**:
8. Brahmo-Samaj (Hindu reform, ~1800s, democratic monotheism)
9. AFSCME-Labor-Union (labor/syndicalist, 1936, delegate democracy, 1.6M members)
10. Mondragon-Cooperative (economic/worker-ownership, 1956, democratic, 80k members)
11. Harvard-University (education/degree authority, 1636, trustee governance, 23k members)
12. Linux-Community (technical/meritocratic, 1991, decentralized, ∞ contributors)

